# Vision

## Mission

Make life palatable. Take the power back.

Technology should serve people without demanding constant migration, subscriptions, or attention. Umbrella should make useful tools easier to combine while preserving the ability to keep them separate, replace them, and recover their history.

## Philosophy

People control their identities, records, relationships, and history. Applications remain independent: their storage, development, releases, and standalone use do not belong to Umbrella. Participation is voluntary and requires no central application approval. Shared capabilities should use open standards and replaceable FOSS-first components.

User-controlled upgrades and reproducible deployments take priority over automatic infrastructure churn. AI assistance is optional. A graphical dashboard may help some users; every essential operation must also be possible headlessly.

## Experience goals

A user should understand what an application exposes before connecting it, select what to share and with whom, and be able to keep applications siloed. Disconnecting should stop future access while preserving standalone operation. Previously shared copies and permanent history require explicit retention and revocation policies; disconnection cannot promise erasure of copies already received.

Saved actions should have understandable, permanent logical history, including revision, tombstone, and restoration. Recovery must be understandable and testable. Simplicity matters more than a large feature catalog.

These are goals. The discovery proof of concept does not yet implement these experiences.
